WARNING! Long post.  Further down is a VERY detailed scheme for an
improved legal system.  It covers judicial and penal pretty thoroughly,
and touches on legislative.  Haven't worked on executive and enforcement yet.
It should be noted that it is entirely my own theory/plan and is the product
of MUCH discussion I have had over the last year with others in various fora.
I am not an expert on law so there are likely many flaws.  Please help me
correct them so I can whip this into a worthy essay.

Note that it is a proposition for an INTERIM legal system, between the
Dark Ages of now, and the future of NO gov't. that I think all of us on
this list long for.  It will, I think, be quite easily adapted to
post-govt legal systems, will aid in bringing about Drexlerian science
courts, and help break the stranglehold of the govt.  If it's implemented.

Note also that this post took more than 5 hours, so I hope you will take
some time to wade thru it and reply thoughtfully (should not be much of a
chore for the calibre of minds on this list :)

If anyone does not get the whole thing due to size, I will be happy to
send it in parts to you.

Misc BSing comes first:

Quoth Alexander Chislenko, verily I saith unto thee:
> 
>   The author notes that in *every* state of U.S. tattoos are prohibited
> for children - usually until 18, in some states until 21, on the grounds
> that until that age people are not mature enough to take such irreversible
> action.

I wonder what The Law's [snicker] opinion of piercing is?  I suspect it is
just as illogical and baseless, and firmly predict that in the majority of
states it is illegal for underagers to seek pro body piercing other than
ears and noses (yes I am hinting directly at genital piercings and the
more dangerous sorts like lip, tongue and cheek, and probably just "wierd"
ones too, like nape, forehead [yes you CAN pierce your forehead!],
finger-webbing, arm, eyebrow, navel, etc.)  To be on the safe side, I also
suspect that they don't have any laws on the books that would punish teens
for getting such piercings, or punish people who do it non-professionally
on their friends or lovers, especially if the piercer is also a teen.

ANYWAY, I strongly agree with Alexander that this sort of moral-based law
is ludicrous and contemptible, and that something ought to be done about
it, if anything can.  My suggestion?  Lobbying to FORCE law making and
court fighting descisions to be based on PROVABLE SCIENTIFIC EVIDENCE.  I
have long proposed this in other discussions in other fora, mainly due to
learning many years ago that legal "proof" and scientific proof have
almost 0 in common.  Legal standards of "proof" and evidence are hideously
illogical and stupid.  Sure many criminals would go free, but many many
innocent people would be spared having their lives suddenly destroyed out
of the blue, and I think the law system would be far far more stable.  It
would also have the secondary (and possibly more important) benefit of
setting a useful precedent that could be used for establishing "science
courts" a la Drexler.  I am not aware of any list consensus on the
importance of such due-process-based scientific inquiry and fact-finding,
but personal I think it is a very good idea if implemented properly.
[aside: some of you may remember that I voted for the Natural Law Party;
the reason was that their brochure stated that they would base policy on
verifiable, proven scientific methodology; that was enough for me!  I now
know that was a lie, and have learned a valuable lesson or 2 or 10.]

>   >   What I think is interesting here is the question of when the child
> becomes mature enough to be able to make responsible decisions on
> tattooing her foreskin ;;-) , taking drugs, driving, etc. 

<chuckle; after some thought that has funny implications "Hi, this is
John, my foreskin.  I tatooed him just there this morning myself." <tugs
poor John's bull-style nosering chain and leads him teary-eyed away>

>   I absolutely refuse accepting the idea that every person suddenly
> becomes sufficiently mature in all respects at exactly the same age.

You betcha!  What a stupid idea.  I think it worked, statistically, at one
time, but since one could probably devise statistically MORE reliable
maturity tests, this seems like the right thing to do.  It could even be
used to get borderline dangerous elderly people off the road who are
otherwise PHYSICALLY hale, since it's been noted that the senile tend to
become more childish as their senility advances [last I heard anyway,
perhaps this is outdated psych?]

> besides, maturity is a relative thing, and we could talk about fuzzy laws,
> etc. - it's getting too complicated to be practical under any judicial
> system.

Agreed, but do you [meaning the list at large] think that a legal system
(both legislative and judicial...what the hell throw in the executive
branch as well, and the pentagon/spy ring circus too) based on MANDATED
scientific method and peer review* that cannot be circumvented (legally)
would work? 

* New idea: have TWO juries, one of typical random folks, and another of
experts in fields relevant to the case, either criminal or civil, agreed
upon by both sides.  The expert jury can declare a mistrial if they
determine that the proceedings violated proper sci. methodology, and can
dismiss the case if they feel that evidence is insufficient.  The judge
can declare a mistrial or dismiss the case if it determines that
proceedings or evidence gathering, etc. did not follow proper LEGAL
procedures, just as judges may do now.

In cases of conflict between expert jury and judge, if one says mistrial
and the other disagrees, the case defaults to mistrial to be safe.  In
cases of dismissal disagreement, the case will be put in arbitration by
ANOTHER, higher up [in case of supreme court, equivalent] judge and
scientific jury, who's decision is either final [or moves up to another
level of arbitration if tied again], or is taken as a recommendation to
the PEER jury, who then decides, I'm not sure which would work better.

In cases where no mistrial or dismissal pops up, the case proceeds as normal.
At the end, the technical jury debates, and makes a RECOMMENDATION to the
peer jury, who then make the final decision.  Alternately, in case this
would be too "leading" to the peer jury, the sci. jury is silent util
the peer jury decides, then both give their verdicts.  In case of a tie
it goes to a higher arbitration court, this time with another PEER jury
as well.  When the verdicts are not tied, the judge announces the total
verdict (just for formality's sake, so it has something to do besides
sit there in a robe), and passes sentence if "guilty" is the hue and cry.

Yet more alternately: the tech jury deliberates and delivers a verdict
on whether the case was sound and scientific, and that only, and the
peer jury decides the guilt or innocence.  I'd think this would be not
so good, since if the tech jury does not call mistrial, by default it
must be a valid trial.


Further, VERY much needed, protections for the suspect, defendant and convict,
and a weeding out of idiotic crap.

1) all obfuscatory language is to be removed from legalese.  ALL court
proceedings must be done in plain [appropriate language here].  This
includes Latinisms, weasel words, overly long big-words-for-big-words-
sake euphemisms (and cacophonisms) that may be replaced with plain
language with no loss of meaning, archaic grammar and syntax, etc.
This is the almost-21st-Century, not the 15th. [I realize I used bwfbws
up there.  However I know YOU will understand them.  An immigrant, ignorant
or illiterate defendant or juror with poor language skills might not.]

2) the legal profession is to be reformed, such that court cases are
treated as solemn, rational affairs.  This will require ameding the bar
codes such that lawyers may be penalized heavily for grandstanding. It is
high time that people's lives and liberties stopped depending upon who's
lawyer is the loudest, most crafty, most expensive or most popular. To
enforce this, all jurors are to be informed to try as much as humanly
possible to disregard all subjectivity and attempts to influence them by
voice tone, appearance, fame of attorney, etc.  And something (I don't
know what, haven't gotten to that) must must must be done to reduce the
cost of representation.  Perhaps some sort of "legal insurance"?

3) the stigma against self-representation must be utterly stamped out.

4) all jurors must pass a battery of intelligence and psych tests. 
Maybe.  I am not too sure any such things could be devised that would be
reliable and unbiased.  SOMETHING needs to be done to weed out morons,
bigots, religious nuts, and pychos.

5) all appeals are to begin IMMEDIATELY following a trial, and the
(possibly temporarily) convicted may not be confined or have property
confiscated util finally convicted. An exception for incarceration would
be if the defendant is demonstrably violent or can be RELIABLY shown to be
likely to flee "justice".

6) property may not be confiscated and sold under any circumstances (other
than evidence gathering -- I referr to seizure of homes, etc.) until ALL
appeals are completed, and no proceeds of such sales may go to law enforce-
ment agencies.  Current policy is producing all sorts of travesties due to
this obvious conflict of interest.

7) in cases where the court finds in favour of the defendant, the
plaintiff (or govt. in criminal cases) must pay the FULL costs of the
trial, including any incidental costs such as lost revenue, reputation
damages, expense of couselling or medical treatment due to effects of
stress from the trial, etc. and must issue a public apology IN THEIR OWN
WORDING (within specified guidelines -- no weaselling out with form
retractions here!).  This will strongly curb the "sue-happy" and wrongful
accusation trends we are plagued with.

8) in cases where the court finds against the defendant, the defendant
must pay the costs, but limited by several rational factors: if the
expense will unduly burden non-guilty family members, business partners, etc
in which cases the govt. foots the bill (this need not cost tax payers any
more that now.  See the dicussion of funding below.)

9) ANY jury member on either jury may DIRECTLY question the witnesses,
judge, defendant, plaintiff, counsel, and other jury members at ANY time
for clarification and elucidation, and may also suggest lines of
questioning that they feel will help them decide.  The judge may override
any such request, but a majority vote of EITHER jury may override THAT.
The time when the avg. citizen was illiterate, backwards, and woefully
ignorant is LONG past.  The time for monopolistic "ownership" of the
"right" to question, by lawyers is LONG past.  The time for the judge
having absolute rule in the courtroom is LONG past.

10) all decisions by the judge against any party for contempt of court or
perjury may be overturned by a majority vote of all jurors from both
juries combined.  Any sentence for such an offense may be appealed, and
sentence suspended until appeals are done (this part may already be true,
but I am under the impression that it is not.)

11) NO ONE but family members and others that the plaintiff and defendant
invite, limited in number, plus necessary functionaries and
representatives of the media (who must use flashless cameras) are to be
present, thus distractions and subconscious effects on the jury due to
apparent opinions (via facial expression, etc) of the audience are lessened.

12) all "expert witnesses" must show reasonable evidence, to be reviewed
by the technical jury, that they are in fact experts. As I understand
present "expert witness" definitions, the name is misleading.  Thus I
propose a re-categorization: eye[or other sensory]witness, virtual witness
(anyone either party thinks has something to contribute to the case, no
verification required), and expert witness (verified).  The concern here
is that the term "expert witness" can be severely misleading.

13) all jurors are to be informed of the Fully Informed Jury Act and given
a thorough explanation of their rights.  FIJA is to be expanded such that
any law that a jury deems wrong it to be immediately placed before the
legislature for examination.

14) ALL amenities are to be removed from prisons besides TV/radio/whatever
comes next that will only tune to news and educational programming.  Hard
labor is to be reinstated, while prison abuses of convicts are to be
stamped out as much as possible.  High school and college education is to
be provided to those who desire it.  It is time that prisons stopped
becoming bizarre homes for the dysfunctional, and became both SERIOUS
punishment and reform centers that produce useful people.  Positivity
should be stressed: You fucked up, but you can get educated, and get REAL
employment and do well.  A lot of psych can be laid on them in a lot of
different ways, particularly thru the medium of games, such as wargames,
which will appeal to many criminals, but wargames in which no one can win
but those who cooperate and respect ethical considerations and the rights
of others (see one of R. A. Wilson's non-fiction works, in which he
describes such a game).  All walls are to be painted pink (this has been
shown to quite strongly reduce violent tendencies, while grey aggravates
